WHAT IS BROKEN — measured on THIS tree, not replayed from take 1:

  python3 scripts/chain_session_check.py  ->  rc 1, RED with 12 problems:

  1. RESTORE drops a store the COLLECT takes (1 problem).
     The collect tar runs with `-C /`, so every member keeps its ABSOLUTE path
     inside the archive. The restore block has two copies, both of which land
     their source under $HOME: the `home/agent` one and the archive-root
     fallback (`cp -a /sessions-restore/. "$HOME/"`). The qoder lanes run with
     `env HOME=/usr/local/lib/qoder-home` (turn_segment.sh:301 and the other
     qoder arms), so their session store is collected as the member
     `usr/local/lib/qoder-home/.qoder` (the last line of the tar list) and is
     then NEVER copied back to /usr/local/lib/qoder-home/.qoder — it comes home
     as $HOME/usr/local/lib/qoder-home/.qoder, where no CLI reads it.
     Measured live in THIS container at boot: /sessions-restore exists and is
     EMPTY (0 entries) while the qoder store it should have fed holds two
     session transcripts
     (/usr/local/lib/qoder-home/.qoder/projects/-workspace/<id>.jsonl,
     174 KB and 582 KB). The store is collected every turn and the seat still
     starts cold: SESSION LAW (Owner 2026-10-21 18:27Z) does not close.

  2. GOOSE-STORE: the collect list names a path current goose never writes
     (1 problem). The tar lists "${HOME#/}/.config/goose/sessions"; goose
     >= 1.5x keeps its session in a SQLite store under
     .local/share/goose/sessions. Both paths now live in the same tar line
     list, so an older goose is still served. `git status`-quiet, because the
     collect ends in `2>/dev/null || true`: tar's "Cannot stat" is swallowed
     every run and the miss is silent.
     Graded against the pinned layout when no store is present in the lane that
     runs the check (the harness prints which it used).

  3. NO-OPT-OUT: every goose lane opts out of the law (10 problems).
     All ten fallback arms call `goose run --no-session -t "$BRIEF"` (lines 309,
     325, 334, 349, 357, 363, 410, 418, 445, 487 of the carrier). The flag's own
     help, measured in this image (`goose run --help`):
         --no-session  Execute commands without creating or using a session
                       file. Useful for automated runs.
     That is the opposite of the Owner's SESSION LAW: a goose seat can never
     resume, by construction. Take 1 found three arms; the rotation has since
     grown the set to ten, which is exactly why this is now a graded count and
     not a prose sentence.

WHAT THE PATCH DOES (three repairs, no other line touched):
  * adds a restore copy for the archive member
    /sessions-restore/usr/local/lib/qoder-home/.qoder -> that same absolute
    path, guarded by `-d` and tolerant of the container's non-root user;
  * adds "${HOME#/}/.local/share/goose/sessions" to the collect list, beside
    the older path;
  * drops `--no-session` from all ten goose arms.

VERIFIED BEFORE SHIPPING (all four on this tree, HEAD 7287a8d):
  * `bash -n .github/scripts/turn_segment.sh` clean after applying;
  * `git apply --check docs/session-loop-fix.patch` clean before applying
    (hence the patch is applicable to the carrier as it stands today);
  * with the hunks applied: `python3 scripts/chain_session_check.py` ->
    rc 0, `SESSION-LOOP CHECK OK — 7 store(s) collected, all restored to their
    own path` (it was rc 1 / 12 problems on the untouched tree);
  * the tree this patch ships from is UNMODIFIED — the hunks were applied in a
    scratch copy, graded, and reverted; `git status` is clean beside this file,
    the new check and the two docs lines.

RISK NOTE (Lead's call, stated honestly):
  * hunk 3 changes how the goose lanes run. If a goose build ever errors without
    --no-session, the `elif` chain falls through to the next lane: the worst case
    is a lane-order change, not a stalled chain.
  * Dropping the flag makes the store EXIST (law satisfied, artifact collected,
    seat-side resume possible); it does not by itself make goose resume a named
    session — `goose run` starts a fresh one. Resuming needs the arm to pass the
    session id, which is a second, separate Lead decision.
